// Imports are scoped PER FILE, not per package (Go spec: the import declaration
// states that the file depends on the imported package).
//
// When multi-file packages first compiled, every file's imports were seeded
// into the one package scope, so the union was visible to all of them and this
// program compiled. That is wrong: a file that uses a package it never imported
// must be rejected, exactly as Go rejects it.
//
// The fix inverts the scope chain — a per-file import scope becomes the PARENT
// of the package scope, so declarations still land in the package scope while a
// lookup falls through to exactly one file's imports.
package main

import "./hygienepkg"

func main() {
	hygienepkg.A()
}
